.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt SHMGET 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm shmget ,
.Nm shmat ,
.Nm shmdt ,
.Nm shmctl
.Nd System V shared memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/shm.h
.Ft int
.Fn shmget "key_t key" "size_t size" "int shmflg"
.Ft void *
.Fn shmat "int shmid" "const void *shmaddr" "int shmflg"
.Ft int
.Fn shmdt "const void *shmaddr"
.Ft int
.Fn shmctl "int shmid" "int cmd" "struct shmid_ds *buf"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A segment is memory of
.Fa size
bytes that each process attaching it maps shared, as on Linux.
Its pages are those of a file on the tmpfs of
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
given when first touched and zeroed.
.Pp
.Fn shmget
returns the id of the segment of
.Fa key ,
or of a new one made with
.Dv IPC_CREAT
in
.Fa shmflg ,
whose low nine bits are its permissions, or always for
.Dv IPC_PRIVATE .
.Dv IPC_CREAT
with
.Dv IPC_EXCL
refuses a key in use.
An id names one segment: its place in a table of 64, plus a sequence
number times 32768, as Linux builds it.
.Pp
.Fn shmat
maps the segment and returns where: at
.Fa shmaddr ,
which must be page aligned unless
.Dv SHM_RND
rounds it down and may not cover a mapping unless
.Dv SHM_REMAP
replaces it, or where the kernel finds room when it is NULL.
.Dv SHM_RDONLY
maps it read-only, and
.Xr mprotect 2
then refuses to make it writable;
.Dv SHM_EXEC
maps it executable.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /maps
names an attachment
.Pa /SYSV Ns Ar key No (deleted) ,
the key in eight hex digits.
.Pp
.Fn shmdt
unmaps the attachment that begins at
.Fa shmaddr .
.Pp
.Fn shmctl
takes
.Dv IPC_STAT ,
which copies the segment's record to
.Fa buf ,
.Dv SHM_STAT
and
.Dv SHM_STAT_ANY ,
the same by the place in the table, returning the id,
.Dv IPC_SET ,
which takes the owner, group and permission bits from
.Fa buf ,
.Dv IPC_RMID ,
.Dv IPC_INFO
and
.Dv SHM_INFO ,
the limits and the use, and
.Dv SHM_LOCK
and
.Dv SHM_UNLOCK ,
which do nothing, since no page is swapped.
.Dv IPC_RMID
removes an unattached segment at once; an attached one is marked
.Dv SHM_DEST
in its mode, its key becomes
.Dv IPC_PRIVATE ,
and it is freed when the table is next read after its last attachment
goes, which is how this kernel, which does not watch an address space's
end, follows Linux's free at the last detach.
.Pp
The attachments counted,
.Va shm_nattch ,
are the regions of every address space that map the segment from its
start.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
has its parent's attachments;
.Xr execve 2
and the end of a process take its own away.
.Ss System call interface
The direct calls are numbers 395
.Pq Fn shmget ,
396
.Pq Fn shmctl ,
397
.Pq Fn shmat
and 398
.Pq Fn shmdt ,
which GNU libc makes; musl reaches the same through
.Xr ipc 2 .
.Fn shmctl
takes the 64-bit layout only, which both libraries ask for with
.Dv IPC_64 .
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm shmget
is call 29,
.Nm shmat
30,
.Nm shmdt
67 and
.Nm shmctl
31, with no
.Nm ipc ;
.Nm shmat
returns the address itself.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn shmget
returns an id,
.Fn shmat
an address, and
.Fn shmdt
and
.Fn shmctl
0, or the highest place in use for the info commands and an id for the
STAT commands; on failure -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
The caller may not read the segment, or write it without
.Dv SHM_RDONLY ;
or its bits do not give what
.Fa shmflg
asks.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
.Dv IPC_CREAT
and
.Dv IPC_EXCL
for a key in use.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A size outside 1 to 4278190079 on i386, or to 18446744073692774399 on
x86-64, or past an existing segment's, an id with
no segment, an unaligned address without
.Dv SHM_RND ,
an address that covers a mapping without
.Dv SHM_REMAP ,
no attachment at
.Fa shmaddr ,
or another command.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
No segment of
.Fa key
and no
.Dv IPC_CREAT .
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
No memory for the segment or the mapping, or for the regions needed to
unmap an attachment.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
All 64 places are taken.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Dv IPC_SET
or
.Dv IPC_RMID
from a caller that neither owns nor made the segment and is not
privileged.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ipc 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr msgget 2 ,
.Xr semget 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
